{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_012Diyj2W76kSoXhiA5GiVNn","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The user wants me to generate detailed predicted data for each \"planned\" experiment in the experiment_plan, based on the \"median\" tier definition provided. I need to provide specific expected results with rationales for why those results would occur in this tier.\n\nLet me go through each planned experiment:\n\n1. exp-001 (sica_expanded_14b) - RUNNING, so I should include predictions\n2. exp-002 (sica_k_ablation_14b) - planned\n3. exp-003 (tiebreak_baseline_analysis) - planned (note says completed but status is planned)\n\n\n4. exp-004 (sica_7b_scaling) - planned\n5. exp-005 (qwq_32b_operating_regime) - planned\n6. exp-006 (c0_diagnostic_validation) - planned\n7. exp-007 (sica_72b_awq) - planned\n8. exp-008 (constraint_quality_case_studies) - planned\n9. exp-009 (overhead_analysis) - planned\n10. exp-010 (sica_mistral_7b) - planned\n11. exp-011 (sica_llama_8b) - planned\n12. exp-012 (gsm8k_pilot_14b) - planned\n13. exp-013 (constraint_ablation) - planned\n14. exp-014 (sica_deep) - planned\n\nNow I need to generate predicted data for each of these experiments based on the median tier characteristics.\n\nThe median tier shows FOLIO gaps of 3-5 percentage points with weak significance, combined gaps around 1-2 points that don't reach significance, and C0 scaling patterns that are noisy but generally favor smaller models. MaxSAT improvements are marginal, and tiebreak win rates hover around 60-70%. Let me work through what exp-001 with its expanded 14B dataset would likely show.\n\nFor the expanded 14B experiment, I'm expecting the FOLIO gap to dilute down to that 3-5 point range at scale while the PW gap stays near zero, giving a combined effect of 1-2 points with C0 estimates around 0.35-0.5. The K ablation experiment should reveal how tiebreak frequency shifts with different K values and how SICA's benefit correlates with those tie patterns. The tiebreak baseline experiment is marked as completed but listed as planned, so I'll predict it lands in that 60-70% win rate range for SICA. For the 7B scaling experiment, weaker models typically show higher C0 values, so I'm anticipating the 7B C0 to fall somewhere in the 0.45-0.6 range with corresponding SICA gaps.\n\nThe 32B QwQ experiment already has the data ready and just needs formatting—C0 comes in at 3.6% with SICA equivalent to SC. The C0 diagnostic validation should show a clear pattern where C0 above 20% correlates with gains and below 5% shows no gains, though the median tier relationship looks noisier. For the 72B AWQ model, stronger performance typically means lower C0, so I'm expecting it to land between 0.25-0.45, though it's not clearly below the 14B results in the median tier. The constraint quality analysis will be qualitative, examining whether the constraints themselves are semantically sound. Finally, the overhead analysis breaks down wall-time costs, with the 60-question run taking around 58 minutes based on the existing data.\n\nNow looking at the remaining experiments: the Mistral 7B and LLaMA 8B cross-family validations should confirm the C0>20% pattern in the median tier, though the gains appear inconsistent across runs. The GSM8K pilot with 14B should show low C0 since the model is already strong on that benchmark, validating C0 as a genuine uncertainty signal. For constraint ablation, I'm comparing MaxSAT against count-only and random-weight approaches, expecting MaxSAT to perform at least as well but with a delta under 2 percentage points. Finally, DeepSeek-R1 as a reasoning model should exhibit the low C0 pattern consistent with the sub-5% threshold.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":13041,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":13041},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"825d69a6-8dd2-4a2c-bb74-4b8dca8f1b0e","uuid":"1d23fe36-de28-4d8c-9151-177ff3a3ffe9"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_012Diyj2W76kSoXhiA5GiVNn","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"```yaml\ntier: median\nnarrative_guidance: \"Claim 1 reframed from 'accuracy improvement' to 'principled interpretable tiebreaking with positive trend on formal-logic domains.' Claim 2 becomes lead — C0 operating regime characterization is primary contribution, validated directionally across 3 families even though per-family SICA-SC deltas are noisy. Claim 3 downgraded from 'continuous predictor' to 'binary diagnostic' (C0>20% = try SICA, C0<5% = skip). MaxSAT framed as 'interpretability + marginal gains.' Title: 'When Does Symbolic Consensus Help?' Target: EMNLP Findings (55-65%).\"\n\nimagined_experiments:\n  - name: \"sica_expanded_14b\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-001\"\n    description: \"PW D5 600 + FOLIO 204 = 804 questions, K=12, T=0.7, Qwen2.5-14B-Instruct\"\n    expected_results:\n      n_total: 804\n      n_folio: 204\n      n_proofwriter: 600\n      sica_accuracy_combined: 0.718\n      sc_accuracy_combined: 0.703\n      sica_sc_gap_combined_pp: 1.5\n      bootstrap_p_combined: 0.19\n      sica_accuracy_folio: 0.784\n      sc_accuracy_folio: 0.745\n      sica_sc_gap_folio_pp: 3.9\n      bootstrap_p_folio: 0.08\n      sica_accuracy_proofwriter: 0.695\n      sc_accuracy_proofwriter: 0.689\n      sica_sc_gap_proofwriter_pp: 0.6\n      bootstrap_p_proofwriter: 0.38\n      c0_multi_candidate_rate: 0.42\n      c0_folio: 0.52\n      c0_proofwriter: 0.38\n      tiebreak_cases: 68\n      tiebreak_sica_wins: 44\n      tiebreak_win_rate: 0.647\n      maxsat_exclusion_rate: 0.031\n      total_constraints_unique: 23800\n      wall_time_hours: 13.2\n    result_rationale: \"At scale, FOLIO retains a real but attenuated gap (~3.9pp vs pilot's 6.7pp) because the pilot's 30 questions were disproportionately tie-heavy; the full 204 includes easier items where SC already converges (C0 drops on easy items). Bootstrap p lands at 0.08 — suggestive but not clean at α=0.05. ProofWriter's closed-world reasoning produces less diverse traces than FOLIO's open-world NLI; most PW gains come from depth-5 items where traces occasionally diverge, but the majority of PW questions show near-unanimous SC votes, yielding a negligible 0.6pp gap (p=0.38). Combined significance is diluted by PW's 600-question mass pulling the average toward zero. The C0 rate of 0.42 confirms the operating regime (>20%) but the per-question C0 variance means many individual questions contribute zero signal. Tiebreak win rate of 64.7% is above random (50%) but below the pilot's 80% (small-sample inflation in pilot).\"\n    tables:\n      - \"T1: Main Results (14B row: FOLIO 78.4 vs 74.5, PW 69.5 vs 68.9, Combined 71.8 vs 70.3)\"\n      - \"T2: Tiebreak Analysis (68 ties, SICA wins 44, SC wins 24, win rate 64.7%)\"\n    figures_description:\n      - \"F2 data point: (C0=0.42, gap=+1.5pp) for combined; (C0=0.52, gap=+3.9pp) for FOLIO subset\"\n    analysis: \"FOLIO is strongest evidence for Claim 1 but p=0.08 requires careful framing — 'trending toward significance' or report one-tailed. Combined result is not significant; paper must not overclaim. ProofWriter contributes to C0 validation (even at C0=0.38, gains are minimal on closed-world tasks) which actually supports the narrative that task formality matters. Key insight for paper: SICA's value concentrates in tie-heavy formal-logic domains, not uniformly across all logic tasks.\"\n\n  - name: \"sica_k_ablation_14b\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-002\"\n    description: \"K sensitivity (K=6,8,12,16,20) on D012's 60 questions, Qwen2.5-14B-Instruct\"\n    expected_results:\n      k6_sica_accuracy: 0.717\n      k6_sc_accuracy: 0.700\n      k6_gap_pp: 1.7\n      k6_c0: 0.40\n      k6_tie_rate: 0.15\n      k8_sica_accuracy: 0.733\n      k8_sc_accuracy: 0.700\n      k8_gap_pp: 3.3\n      k8_c0: 0.47\n      k8_tie_rate: 0.22\n      k12_sica_accuracy: 0.733\n      k12_sc_accuracy: 0.700\n      k12_gap_pp: 3.3\n      k12_c0: 0.53\n      k12_tie_rate: 0.25\n      k16_sica_accuracy: 0.750\n      k16_sc_accuracy: 0.717\n      k16_gap_pp: 3.3\n      k16_c0: 0.57\n      k16_tie_rate: 0.18\n      k20_sica_accuracy: 0.750\n      k20_sc_accuracy: 0.733\n      k20_gap_pp: 1.7\n      k20_c0: 0.60\n      k20_tie_rate: 0.13\n      wall_time_hours: 4.5\n    result_rationale: \"C0 increases monotonically with K (more samples → more chance of diverse answers), but SICA-SC gap does not scale linearly. At K=6, too few traces means ties are rare (15%) and SC often has a clear majority — SICA has few opportunities to intervene. K=8 and K=12 hit the sweet spot: even K values create more exact ties, giving SICA maximum leverage. At K=16/20, C0 rises further but the majority winner becomes more stable (law of large numbers); ties actually decrease because the leading answer accumulates enough margin. The gap at K=20 shrinks because SC's majority is robust enough that tiebreaking matters less. This produces an inverted-U pattern peaking around K=8-12. Critically, odd K values (not tested here but inferrable) would show fewer ties, further supporting that SICA's primary mechanism is tiebreaking rather than general accuracy improvement.\"\n    tables:\n      - \"T3: K Sensitivity (K vs gap, C0, tie_rate — shows inverted-U peak at K=8-12)\"\n    figures_description:\n      - \"F4: K curve — x-axis K, dual y-axis showing SICA-SC gap (bars) and tie rate (line). Peak gap at K=8-12, declining at K=20.\"\n    analysis: \"The inverted-U supports the 'principled tiebreaker' narrative: SICA's value is mechanistically tied to tie frequency, not a general accuracy boost. Paper should emphasize K=8-12 as practical recommendation. The fact that gap doesn't grow unboundedly with K is actually a feature for the 'operating regime' story — it shows SICA's contribution is bounded and predictable.\"\n\n  - name: \"tiebreak_baseline_analysis\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-003\"\n    description: \"SICA vs SC-random vs SC-deterministic on all available data. CPU reanalysis.\"\n    expected_results:\n      n_tiebreak_cases_d012: 5\n      sica_tiebreak_accuracy: 0.80\n      random_tiebreak_accuracy_mean: 0.50\n      alphabetical_tiebreak_accuracy: 0.60\n      first_seen_tiebreak_accuracy: 0.40\n      n_tiebreak_cases_expanded: 68\n      sica_tiebreak_accuracy_expanded: 0.647\n      random_tiebreak_accuracy_expanded_mean: 0.485\n      sica_vs_random_p_expanded: 0.035\n      overall_sica_accuracy_with_random_baseline: 0.710\n      sica_minus_random_baseline_gap_pp: 0.8\n    result_rationale: \"On the original D012 5 ties, SICA's 80% (4/5) is consistent with pilot data. On expanded 68 ties, SICA's advantage regresses toward a more realistic 64.7% — still meaningfully above random's ~48.5% (random doesn't hit exactly 50% due to answer distribution asymmetry in the dataset). The critical comparison is: if SC uses random tiebreaking instead of alphabetical, it already captures some of the 'gap' attributed to SICA. The overall SICA-vs-SC-random gap shrinks to ~0.8pp (from 1.5pp vs deterministic), because random tiebreak is correct ~50% of the time on ties vs SICA's ~65%. This means roughly half of SICA's overall accuracy advantage comes from better tiebreaking specifically, and the other half from ties where SICA's constraint-weighted answer happens to differ from SC's majority (non-tie cases where MaxSAT exclusion changes the winner). The p=0.035 on tie-only comparison is the paper's cleanest statistical result.\"\n    tables:\n      - \"T2 extended: Tiebreak comparison table (SICA 64.7%, Random 48.5%, Alphabetical 52.9%, First-seen 45.6%)\"\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Bar chart comparing tiebreak strategies on 68 tie cases with 95% CI error bars\"\n    analysis: \"This is both the strongest and most modest result: SICA is a statistically significant better tiebreaker (p=0.035 on ties), but overall accuracy gain over a fair random baseline is small (~0.8pp). Paper must be honest about this decomposition. Reframe: 'SICA provides principled, interpretable tiebreaking with significant per-tie advantage, contributing a modest but consistent overall accuracy improvement.' The interpretability angle (you can inspect WHY SICA chose an answer via constraints) becomes the differentiator over random.\"\n\n  - name: \"sica_7b_scaling\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-004\"\n    description: \"Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct on D012 60 questions, K=12, T=0.7\"\n    expected_results:\n      sica_accuracy: 0.650\n      sc_accuracy: 0.617\n      sica_sc_gap_pp: 3.3\n      c0_multi_candidate_rate: 0.55\n      bootstrap_p: 0.18\n      folio_sica: 0.700\n      folio_sc: 0.633\n      folio_gap_pp: 6.7\n      proofwriter_sica: 0.619\n      proofwriter_sc: 0.607\n      proofwriter_gap_pp: 1.2\n      tiebreak_cases: 11\n      tiebreak_win_rate: 0.636\n      constraint_extraction_rate: 0.92\n      wall_time_hours: 1.8\n    result_rationale: \"7B is weaker than 14B (SC baseline drops from 70% to ~62%), producing more diverse outputs (C0 rises from 0.53 to 0.55). The C0 increase is real but modest — the 7B model is weaker but not dramatically so on these tasks; it still often converges on common answers. The SICA gap of 3.3pp matches the median expectation — higher C0 creates more intervention opportunities, but 7B's constraint extraction quality is lower (92% vs 100% for 14B), partially offsetting the diversity advantage. FOLIO shows the clearest gain (+6.7pp) because open-world NLI with a 3-class answer space maximizes tie probability for a confused model. However, bootstrap p=0.18 on 60 questions means none of this is individually significant — only the cross-model pattern matters. The key data point is: C0(7B)=0.55 > C0(14B)=0.53, confirming the directional scaling trend even if the magnitude is modest.\"\n    tables:\n      - \"T1: 7B row (FOLIO 70.0 vs 63.3, PW 61.9 vs 60.7, Combined 65.0 vs 61.7)\"\n    figures_description:\n      - \"F2 data point: (C0=0.55, gap=+3.3pp) — above and right of 14B point, confirms positive correlation\"\n    analysis: \"7B confirms 'weaker model → higher C0 → SICA helps more' directionally. But the C0 jump (0.53→0.55) is smaller than hoped — median tier says the scaling trend is noisy. The 7B point helps the F2 scatter plot but doesn't create a clean linear relationship. For the paper: include in T1 and F2, note that within-family scaling shows the expected direction but the gradient is shallow for adjacent sizes.\"\n\n  - name: \"qwq_32b_operating_regime\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-005\"\n    description: \"Format existing QwQ-32B data for Operating Regime Analysis section\"\n    expected_results:\n      c0_combined: 0.036\n      c0_aime: 0.0\n      c0_proofwriter: 0.1875\n      c0_folio: 0.0\n      sica_sc_gap_pp: 0.0\n      n_problems_total: 48\n      n_aime: 22\n      n_proofwriter: 16\n      n_folio: 10\n      reasoning_model_conclusion: \"C0<5% → SICA≡SC structurally\"\n    result_rationale: \"QwQ-32B's reasoning chains are so self-consistent that sampling temperature has negligible effect on final answers. The model's extended chain-of-thought process (often 2000+ tokens) acts as an internal consistency mechanism — by the time it reaches an answer, it has self-corrected any stochastic variation. C0=3.6% means only ~2 out of 48 problems produced any answer diversity at all, and even those had overwhelming majorities. SICA is structurally identical to SC because there are no ties to break and no minority answers for MaxSAT to potentially elevate. This is the 'degenerate regime' — the method's mechanism (resolving disagreement) has no disagreement to resolve. The key insight is that this is model-class-dependent (reasoning vs instruction), not task-dependent (same FOLIO/PW tasks show high C0 on 14B instruction model).\"\n    tables:\n      - \"T1: QwQ-32B row (all gaps = 0.0pp, C0 = 3.6%)\"\n    figures_description:\n      - \"F2 data point: (C0=0.036, gap=0.0pp) — bottom-left corner, clearly in 'degenerate regime'\"\n      - \"F3 heatmap: QwQ-32B occupies the C0<5% zone across all tested domains\"\n    analysis: \"This is Claim 2's strongest evidence in the paper. The contrast between 14B-Instruct (C0=53%, gap=+3.3pp) and QwQ-32B (C0=3.6%, gap=0pp) on the SAME tasks demonstrates that C0 is the governing variable, not task difficulty. Present as: 'reasoning models self-consistent → SICA redundant; instruction models diverse → SICA helps.' This frames the negative result as a positive insight about operating regimes.\"\n\n  - name: \"c0_diagnostic_validation\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-006\"\n    description: \"C0 vs SICA-SC gap scatter plot across all models and K values. CPU analysis.\"\n    expected_results:\n      n_data_points: 14\n      pearson_r: 0.58\n      spearman_rho: 0.64\n      c0_threshold_20_precision: 0.75\n      c0_threshold_20_recall: 1.0\n      c0_below_5_all_zero_gap: true\n      c0_above_20_positive_gap_rate: 0.75\n      binary_classification_accuracy: 0.857\n      auc_roc: 0.82\n    result_rationale: \"With ~14 data points (7B, 14B, 72B on 60q; 14B on 804q; QwQ on 48q; Mistral on 60q; LLaMA on 60q; DeepSeek-R1 on 60q; plus K-ablation variants), the correlation is positive but noisy (r=0.58). The noise comes from: (1) Mistral and LLaMA having high C0 but inconsistent gaps (one shows gain, one flat — median tier assumption), (2) K-ablation points clustering in a narrow C0 range, (3) PW subset having moderate C0 but near-zero gap (task formality confound). However, the BINARY diagnostic works well: C0<5% always means gap≈0 (reasoning models), and C0>20% means gap>0 in 75% of cases. The 25% failure rate (C0>20% but gap≈0) corresponds to cases where high diversity exists but constraint extraction quality is poor or the task doesn't benefit from symbolic reasoning (e.g., one cross-family model). Spearman (rank correlation) beats Pearson because the relationship is monotone but non-linear — there's a threshold effect rather than a smooth gradient.\"\n    tables:\n      - \"Appendix table: All (model, dataset, K, C0, gap) data points\"\n    figures_description:\n      - \"F2 (hero figure): Scatter plot C0 (x) vs gap (y) with color=model family, shape=reasoning/instruction. Two regime zones shaded: green (C0>20%, 'SICA regime') and red (C0<5%, 'degenerate regime'). Gray zone (5-20%) labeled 'uncertain.'\"\n    analysis: \"The binary diagnostic (C0>20% → try SICA, C0<5% → skip) is the paper's Claim 3 contribution. In median tier, it works as a diagnostic but NOT as a continuous predictor — the scatter has too much variance for a regression line to be useful. Paper should present the binary threshold clearly and acknowledge that within the 'SICA regime' (C0>20%), the magnitude of gain is task-dependent and not precisely predicted by C0 alone. This downgrades Claim 3 from 'continuous predictor' to 'binary diagnostic' as specified in the tier definition.\"\n\n  - name: \"sica_72b_awq\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-007\"\n    description: \"Qwen2.5-72B-Instruct AWQ 4-bit on D012 60 questions, K=12, T=0.7\"\n    expected_results:\n      sica_accuracy: 0.783\n      sc_accuracy: 0.767\n      sica_sc_gap_pp: 1.6\n      c0_multi_candidate_rate: 0.37\n      bootstrap_p: 0.35\n      folio_sica: 0.867\n      folio_sc: 0.833\n      folio_gap_pp: 3.4\n      proofwriter_sica: 0.738\n      proofwriter_sc: 0.738\n      proofwriter_gap_pp: 0.0\n      tiebreak_cases: 4\n      tiebreak_win_rate: 0.75\n      constraint_extraction_rate: 1.0\n      wall_time_hours: 3.8\n    result_rationale: \"72B is stronger than 14B (SC baseline 76.7% vs 70%), so it converges more often — but NOT dramatically more. C0=0.37 is below 14B's 0.53 but still well above the 20% threshold. In median tier, '72B not clearly below 14B' means the C0 drop is real but modest (0.53→0.37, not 0.53→0.15). This happens because 72B is still an instruction model — it doesn't have the reasoning model's self-consistency mechanism, just better initial accuracy. It still produces diverse outputs on genuinely ambiguous items. The SICA gap is small (1.6pp) because: (1) fewer ties with 72B's stronger majorities, (2) on PW, 72B is so accurate that there's no room for improvement. FOLIO still shows a gap (+3.4pp) because even 72B finds open-world NLI challenging enough to produce diverse traces. The scaling story becomes: 7B (C0=0.55) > 14B (C0=0.53) > 72B (C0=0.37) — directionally correct but the 7B-14B gap is tiny while the 14B-72B gap is larger. This is 'noisy' because you'd expect a more uniform gradient.\"\n    tables:\n      - \"T1: 72B row (FOLIO 86.7 vs 83.3, PW 73.8 vs 73.8, Combined 78.3 vs 76.7)\"\n    figures_description:\n      - \"F2 data point: (C0=0.37, gap=+1.6pp) — between 14B and QwQ, confirms within-family scaling\"\n    analysis: \"72B data creates the within-Qwen scaling dimension for the paper: 7B→14B→72B shows C0 decreasing and gap decreasing. But the pattern is noisy (7B and 14B are close, then 72B drops more). This is enough for a 'directional trend' claim but not a clean power law. Present as evidence that stronger instruction models approach but don't reach the reasoning model's degenerate regime. The 72B-AWQ quantization is a potential confound to acknowledge in limitations (4-bit may slightly increase randomness vs full precision).\"\n\n  - name: \"constraint_quality_case_studies\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-008\"\n    description: \"Qualitative analysis of constraints on 5-10 representative questions from exp-001\"\n    expected_results:\n      n_cases_analyzed: 8\n      semantically_meaningful_rate: 0.75\n      excluded_constraints_error_rate: 0.83\n      constraint_types: [\"entailment_chain\", \"negation\", \"universal_quantifier\", \"conditional\", \"disjunction\"]\n      avg_constraints_per_trace: 4.2\n      avg_unique_constraints_per_problem: 14.8\n      failure_modes: [\"over-literal parsing\", \"scope ambiguity in quantifiers\", \"implicit premises missed\"]\n    result_rationale: \"Constraint extraction from 14B traces is generally meaningful because FOLIO premises are already semi-formal (natural language with clear logical structure). ~75% of extracted constraints correctly capture the intended logical relationship. The 25% failures come from: (1) overly literal parsing of hedging language ('might', 'usually'), (2) quantifier scope ambiguity in nested sentences, (3) implicit common-sense premises that the model encodes but the extractor can't formalize. Excluded constraints (those MaxSAT deems unsatisfiable with the rest) correspond to reasoning errors ~83% of the time — this validates the MaxSAT mechanism as genuinely detecting flawed reasoning rather than randomly dropping constraints. The remaining 17% of exclusions are valid constraints that happen to conflict with the majority due to alternative valid interpretations.\"\n    tables: []\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Figure (appendix): 3 annotated examples showing constraint extraction → MaxSAT → answer selection pipeline on specific FOLIO questions\"\n    analysis: \"Case studies serve the interpretability narrative. Even if accuracy gains are modest, showing that MaxSAT exclusion corresponds to actual reasoning errors (83% of the time) validates the mechanism as principled rather than lucky. This supports reframing from 'accuracy tool' to 'interpretable reasoning aggregation.' Include 2-3 cherry-picked examples in main paper §4, full set in appendix.\"\n\n  - name: \"overhead_analysis\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-009\"\n    description: \"Wall-time breakdown per question from exp-001 logs\"\n    expected_results:\n      avg_total_time_per_question_s: 59.1\n      avg_generation_time_s: 48.3\n      avg_extraction_time_s: 8.4\n      avg_maxsat_time_s: 2.4\n      generation_fraction: 0.817\n      extraction_fraction: 0.142\n      maxsat_fraction: 0.041\n      overhead_vs_sc_percent: 22.4\n      sc_only_time_per_question_s: 48.3\n      batch_throughput_questions_per_hour: 61\n    result_rationale: \"Generation dominates (82%) because K=12 traces each require a full forward pass through the 14B model. Constraint extraction adds ~8.4s per question (LLM-based extraction of FOL from 12 traces, batched). MaxSAT solving is negligible (~2.4s, Z3 on small instances). Total overhead vs pure SC is ~22% — SC would take 48.3s (just generation), SICA adds 10.8s for extraction+solving. This is modest for a research prototype but worth noting. The extraction step could be parallelized (currently sequential over traces) or replaced with a smaller model, suggesting practical deployment could reduce overhead to <10%. Generation time dominates because vLLM batches efficiently but 12 traces × 14B parameters × ~500 output tokens each is substantial.\"\n    tables:\n      - \"T4: Overhead breakdown (Generation 48.3s/81.7%, Extraction 8.4s/14.2%, MaxSAT 2.4s/4.1%, Total overhead +22.4%)\"\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Stacked bar chart: time breakdown per stage, with annotation showing SC-only baseline\"\n    analysis: \"22% overhead is defensible for EMNLP — it's not 10x. Frame as: 'SICA adds modest overhead concentrated in constraint extraction (parallelizable), while MaxSAT solving is negligible. The primary cost is generation (shared with SC). Practical deployment with a dedicated extraction model could reduce overhead to <10%.' Reviewer concern addressed: the overhead IS worth it if you're in the C0>20% regime and want interpretable tiebreaking.\"\n\n  - name: \"sica_mistral_7b\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-010\"\n    description: \"Mistral-7B-Instruct-v0.3 on D012 60 questions, K=12, T=0.7\"\n    expected_results:\n      sica_accuracy: 0.583\n      sc_accuracy: 0.567\n      sica_sc_gap_pp: 1.6\n      c0_multi_candidate_rate: 0.35\n      bootstrap_p: 0.38\n      folio_sica: 0.600\n      folio_sc: 0.567\n      folio_gap_pp: 3.3\n      proofwriter_sica: 0.571\n      proofwriter_sc: 0.571\n      proofwriter_gap_pp: 0.0\n      tiebreak_cases: 6\n      tiebreak_win_rate: 0.667\n      constraint_extraction_rate: 0.85\n      wall_time_hours: 1.3\n    result_rationale: \"Mistral-7B is the 'one flat' family in the median tier's 'one shows gain, one flat' cross-family pattern. C0=0.35 confirms C0>20% holds outside Qwen (critical for Claim 2), but the SICA-SC gap is small and noisy (+1.6pp, p=0.38). This happens because Mistral's constraint extraction quality is lower (85% vs Qwen's 100%) — the model produces less structured reasoning traces, leading to more extraction failures and lower-quality constraints. When MaxSAT operates on noisy constraints, its tiebreaking advantage is partially offset by occasional wrong exclusions. FOLIO still shows a positive direction (+3.3pp) but PW is flat. The key takeaway is: C0 pattern generalizes (Mistral instruction model produces diverse outputs), but SICA's benefit depends on BOTH diversity AND extraction quality. Mistral's shorter, less structured traces hurt extraction.\"\n    tables:\n      - \"T1: Mistral-7B row (FOLIO 60.0 vs 56.7, PW 57.1 vs 57.1, Combined 58.3 vs 56.7)\"\n    figures_description:\n      - \"F2 data point: (C0=0.35, gap=+1.6pp) — in the 'SICA regime' by C0 but low gap, contributing to scatter\"\n    analysis: \"Mistral confirms C0>20% across families (critical) but shows that C0 is necessary-not-sufficient for SICA gains. The paper should acknowledge: 'Cross-family C0 pattern holds, but per-family SICA-SC delta varies with constraint extraction quality.' This is honest and actually strengthens the analysis paper framing — it identifies a second prerequisite beyond C0 (extraction quality). For the escalation decision: gap>0 but p>0.2 on 60 questions — per the plan note, could expand to 200+ questions, but median tier assumes we don't (GPU budget constraint or decision that the 60-question C0 confirmation is sufficient).\"\n\n  - name: \"sica_llama_8b\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-011\"\n    description: \"LLaMA-3.1-8B-Instruct on D012 60 questions, K=12, T=0.7\"\n    expected_results:\n      sica_accuracy: 0.617\n      sc_accuracy: 0.583\n      sica_sc_gap_pp: 3.4\n      c0_multi_candidate_rate: 0.40\n      bootstrap_p: 0.14\n      folio_sica: 0.667\n      folio_sc: 0.600\n      folio_gap_pp: 6.7\n      proofwriter_sica: 0.583\n      proofwriter_sc: 0.571\n      proofwriter_gap_pp: 1.2\n      tiebreak_cases: 9\n      tiebreak_win_rate: 0.667\n      constraint_extraction_rate: 0.93\n      wall_time_hours: 1.4\n    result_rationale: \"LLaMA-8B is the 'one shows gain' family in the median tier's cross-family pattern. C0=0.40 confirms diversity, and the gap (+3.4pp) is comparable to Qwen-14B's pilot result. LLaMA's instruction tuning produces reasonably structured outputs (extraction rate 93%), and at 8B parameters it's weak enough on FOLIO to produce genuine disagreement. The FOLIO gap of +6.7pp looks impressive but with only 30 FOLIO questions it's not significant (p=0.14 for combined). The key difference vs Mistral: LLaMA's traces are more structured (Meta's instruction tuning format includes clearer step markers), enabling better constraint extraction. This makes LLaMA the second 'positive signal' family alongside Qwen, while Mistral is the 'C0 holds but gains are flat' family. Together they support: 'C0 pattern generalizes; SICA benefit depends on both C0 and trace quality.'\"\n    tables:\n      - \"T1: LLaMA-8B row (FOLIO 66.7 vs 60.0, PW 58.3 vs 57.1, Combined 61.7 vs 58.3)\"\n    figures_description:\n      - \"F2 data point: (C0=0.40, gap=+3.4pp) — stronger signal than Mistral, closer to Qwen-14B's position\"\n    analysis: \"LLaMA provides the second positive cross-family confirmation. The 3-family narrative becomes: 'All three instruction-model families show C0>20% (operating regime confirmed), with two of three showing positive SICA-SC gaps and the third showing directional but flat improvement.' This is sufficient for EMNLP Findings — not every family needs significant gains if the C0 pattern is the primary claim. Paper frames Mistral's flat result as 'extraction quality moderates effectiveness' rather than as a failure.\"\n\n  - name: \"gsm8k_pilot_14b\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-012\"\n    description: \"Qwen2.5-14B on GSM8K 30 questions (K=12, T=0.7). Cross-domain C0 test.\"\n    expected_results:\n      sica_accuracy: 0.867\n      sc_accuracy: 0.867\n      sica_sc_gap_pp: 0.0\n      c0_multi_candidate_rate: 0.10\n      n_problems: 30\n      tiebreak_cases: 0\n      constraint_extraction_rate: 0.97\n      sc_baseline_accuracy: 0.867\n      wall_time_hours: 0.9\n    result_rationale: \"Qwen2.5-14B is strong on GSM8K (baseline ~87% with SC@12). The open-answer-space (numeric) combined with high model competence means the model almost always converges on the correct numerical answer. C0=10% — only 3 out of 30 questions produce any answer diversity, and those are the hardest items where the model sometimes makes arithmetic errors. With C0=10% (below 20% threshold but above 5%), this lands in the 'uncertain zone' — not quite degenerate but not clearly in SICA's operating regime. Zero ties means SICA has no mechanism to help. Gap is 0pp. This validates C0 as a cross-domain diagnostic: on tasks where the model is confident (high accuracy), C0 is low and SICA is unnecessary. The result also shows C0 reflects task-relative confidence, not answer-space properties (math has infinite answer space but low C0 when the model is competent).\"\n    tables:\n      - \"Appendix table: GSM8K pilot (C0=10%, gap=0pp, SC accuracy=86.7%)\"\n    figures_description:\n      - \"F2 data point: (C0=0.10, gap=0.0pp) — in the 'uncertain/low' zone, supporting C0 threshold narrative\"\n      - \"F3 heatmap: GSM8K cell in the 'C0<20%' region for 14B\"\n    analysis: \"GSM8K confirms that C0 is low when model competence is high, regardless of answer-space type. This is important for the paper's claim that C0 reflects 'task-relative model confidence.' Include in appendix as cross-domain validation. The result also pre-empts reviewer concern 'only works on restricted answer spaces' — we tested open-answer math and found C0 naturally low (model doesn't need help), which is exactly the predicted behavior.\"\n\n  - name: \"constraint_ablation\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-013\"\n    description: \"CPU ablation: SICA-MaxSAT vs SICA-count-only vs SICA-random-weight on exp-001 data\"\n    expected_results:\n      maxsat_accuracy_combined: 0.718\n      count_only_accuracy_combined: 0.711\n      random_weight_accuracy_combined: 0.706\n      maxsat_vs_countonly_gap_pp: 0.7\n      maxsat_vs_random_gap_pp: 1.2\n      countonly_vs_sc_gap_pp: 0.8\n      maxsat_folio_accuracy: 0.784\n      count_only_folio_accuracy: 0.770\n      maxsat_vs_countonly_folio_pp: 1.4\n      tiebreak_agreement_maxsat_countonly: 0.78\n      n_problems: 804\n    result_rationale: \"In the median tier, MaxSAT is marginally better than count-only but the delta is <2pp. This happens because on most tie cases, the constraint count already correlates with the correct answer (more constraints → more reasoning traces support it → more likely correct). MaxSAT adds value only when constraint QUALITY matters — i.e., when a few high-quality constraints for the correct answer outweigh many low-quality constraints for the wrong answer. On 804 questions with 68 ties, only ~15 cases show this pattern, yielding the marginal 0.7pp overall difference. Count-only still beats SC (by 0.8pp) because even raw constraint counting provides better tiebreaking than arbitrary rules. Random-weight performs worst because it destroys the informative signal entirely. The FOLIO subset shows the clearest MaxSAT advantage (+1.4pp over count-only) because formal-logic constraints have clearer satisfiability structure.\"\n    tables:\n      - \"Appendix table: Constraint Ablation (MaxSAT 71.8%, Count-only 71.1%, Random-weight 70.6%, SC 70.3%)\"\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Bar chart: accuracy by aggregation method with error bars, showing MaxSAT ≥ Count-only ≥ Random ≥ SC\"\n    analysis: \"MaxSAT is NOT essential for the core accuracy claim — count-only gets you 70% of the way there. But MaxSAT provides: (1) marginal additional accuracy, (2) interpretability (you can inspect which constraints were satisfied/excluded), (3) formal grounding (satisfiability checking is principled). Paper frames this as: 'MaxSAT provides interpretability and marginal accuracy gains over simpler alternatives. The primary contribution is the constraint-based framework and C0 diagnostic, not the specific solver.' This defuses the 'over-engineering' attack while preserving the intellectual contribution.\"\n\n  - name: \"sica_deepseek_r1_llama_8b\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-014\"\n    description: \"DeepSeek-R1-Distill-LLaMA-8B on D012 60 questions, K=12, T=0.7\"\n    expected_results:\n      sica_accuracy: 0.683\n      sc_accuracy: 0.683\n      sica_sc_gap_pp: 0.0\n      c0_multi_candidate_rate: 0.05\n      bootstrap_p: null\n      folio_sica: 0.700\n      folio_sc: 0.700\n      folio_gap_pp: 0.0\n      proofwriter_sica: 0.667\n      proofwriter_sc: 0.667\n      proofwriter_gap_pp: 0.0\n      tiebreak_cases: 0\n      tiebreak_win_rate: null\n      constraint_extraction_rate: 0.95\n      wall_time_hours: 1.8\n    result_rationale: \"DeepSeek-R1-Distill-LLaMA-8B is a reasoning model (distilled from R1) despite its smaller size. Like QwQ-32B, it has internalized extended chain-of-thought that produces self-consistent outputs. C0=5% (borderline, at the threshold) — only 3 out of 60 questions show any diversity, and those have overwhelming majorities. The key difference from QwQ-32B: DeepSeek-R1 has a LLaMA base architecture, eliminating the 'still Qwen family' attack on the QwQ data. Combined with QwQ-32B (C0=3.6%), this establishes: reasoning models as a CLASS show C0<5%, regardless of base architecture (Qwen or LLaMA) or scale (8B or 32B). SICA≡SC structurally. SC accuracy of 68.3% is higher than LLaMA-8B-Instruct's 58.3% despite same parameter count — reasoning distillation significantly improves accuracy while eliminating diversity. This is the clean 'reasoning vs instruction' contrast within the same architecture family.\"\n    tables:\n      - \"T1: DeepSeek-R1-LLaMA-8B row (all gaps = 0.0pp, C0 = 5%)\"\n    figures_description:\n      - \"F2 data point: (C0=0.05, gap=0.0pp) — at the boundary of 'degenerate regime', confirming reasoning model pattern\"\n      - \"F3 heatmap: DeepSeek-R1 occupies same low-C0 zone as QwQ-32B\"\n    analysis: \"DeepSeek-R1 is the second reasoning model confirmation. Together with QwQ-32B, establishes: reasoning models (any architecture, any scale) → C0<5% → SICA redundant. The contrast with LLaMA-8B-Instruct (same base, C0=40%, gap=+3.4pp) is particularly powerful: same architecture, instruction tuning vs reasoning distillation, completely different operating regime. This becomes a key comparison pair in the paper: 'LLaMA-8B-Instruct (C0=40%, +3.4pp) vs DeepSeek-R1-LLaMA-8B (C0=5%, 0pp) — same architecture, different training paradigm, opposite SICA behavior.'\"\n```"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":13041,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":13041},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"825d69a6-8dd2-4a2c-bb74-4b8dca8f1b0e","uuid":"b82aba45-35a7-48f3-9a71-b899e9fc2aa8"}
{"type":"result","subtype":"success","is_error":false,"api_error_status":null,"duration_ms":243977,"duration_api_ms":245496,"num_turns":1,"result":"```yaml\ntier: median\nnarrative_guidance: \"Claim 1 reframed from 'accuracy improvement' to 'principled interpretable tiebreaking with positive trend on formal-logic domains.' Claim 2 becomes lead — C0 operating regime characterization is primary contribution, validated directionally across 3 families even though per-family SICA-SC deltas are noisy. Claim 3 downgraded from 'continuous predictor' to 'binary diagnostic' (C0>20% = try SICA, C0<5% = skip). MaxSAT framed as 'interpretability + marginal gains.' Title: 'When Does Symbolic Consensus Help?' Target: EMNLP Findings (55-65%).\"\n\nimagined_experiments:\n  - name: \"sica_expanded_14b\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-001\"\n    description: \"PW D5 600 + FOLIO 204 = 804 questions, K=12, T=0.7, Qwen2.5-14B-Instruct\"\n    expected_results:\n      n_total: 804\n      n_folio: 204\n      n_proofwriter: 600\n      sica_accuracy_combined: 0.718\n      sc_accuracy_combined: 0.703\n      sica_sc_gap_combined_pp: 1.5\n      bootstrap_p_combined: 0.19\n      sica_accuracy_folio: 0.784\n      sc_accuracy_folio: 0.745\n      sica_sc_gap_folio_pp: 3.9\n      bootstrap_p_folio: 0.08\n      sica_accuracy_proofwriter: 0.695\n      sc_accuracy_proofwriter: 0.689\n      sica_sc_gap_proofwriter_pp: 0.6\n      bootstrap_p_proofwriter: 0.38\n      c0_multi_candidate_rate: 0.42\n      c0_folio: 0.52\n      c0_proofwriter: 0.38\n      tiebreak_cases: 68\n      tiebreak_sica_wins: 44\n      tiebreak_win_rate: 0.647\n      maxsat_exclusion_rate: 0.031\n      total_constraints_unique: 23800\n      wall_time_hours: 13.2\n    result_rationale: \"At scale, FOLIO retains a real but attenuated gap (~3.9pp vs pilot's 6.7pp) because the pilot's 30 questions were disproportionately tie-heavy; the full 204 includes easier items where SC already converges (C0 drops on easy items). Bootstrap p lands at 0.08 — suggestive but not clean at α=0.05. ProofWriter's closed-world reasoning produces less diverse traces than FOLIO's open-world NLI; most PW gains come from depth-5 items where traces occasionally diverge, but the majority of PW questions show near-unanimous SC votes, yielding a negligible 0.6pp gap (p=0.38). Combined significance is diluted by PW's 600-question mass pulling the average toward zero. The C0 rate of 0.42 confirms the operating regime (>20%) but the per-question C0 variance means many individual questions contribute zero signal. Tiebreak win rate of 64.7% is above random (50%) but below the pilot's 80% (small-sample inflation in pilot).\"\n    tables:\n      - \"T1: Main Results (14B row: FOLIO 78.4 vs 74.5, PW 69.5 vs 68.9, Combined 71.8 vs 70.3)\"\n      - \"T2: Tiebreak Analysis (68 ties, SICA wins 44, SC wins 24, win rate 64.7%)\"\n    figures_description:\n      - \"F2 data point: (C0=0.42, gap=+1.5pp) for combined; (C0=0.52, gap=+3.9pp) for FOLIO subset\"\n    analysis: \"FOLIO is strongest evidence for Claim 1 but p=0.08 requires careful framing — 'trending toward significance' or report one-tailed. Combined result is not significant; paper must not overclaim. ProofWriter contributes to C0 validation (even at C0=0.38, gains are minimal on closed-world tasks) which actually supports the narrative that task formality matters. Key insight for paper: SICA's value concentrates in tie-heavy formal-logic domains, not uniformly across all logic tasks.\"\n\n  - name: \"sica_k_ablation_14b\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-002\"\n    description: \"K sensitivity (K=6,8,12,16,20) on D012's 60 questions, Qwen2.5-14B-Instruct\"\n    expected_results:\n      k6_sica_accuracy: 0.717\n      k6_sc_accuracy: 0.700\n      k6_gap_pp: 1.7\n      k6_c0: 0.40\n      k6_tie_rate: 0.15\n      k8_sica_accuracy: 0.733\n      k8_sc_accuracy: 0.700\n      k8_gap_pp: 3.3\n      k8_c0: 0.47\n      k8_tie_rate: 0.22\n      k12_sica_accuracy: 0.733\n      k12_sc_accuracy: 0.700\n      k12_gap_pp: 3.3\n      k12_c0: 0.53\n      k12_tie_rate: 0.25\n      k16_sica_accuracy: 0.750\n      k16_sc_accuracy: 0.717\n      k16_gap_pp: 3.3\n      k16_c0: 0.57\n      k16_tie_rate: 0.18\n      k20_sica_accuracy: 0.750\n      k20_sc_accuracy: 0.733\n      k20_gap_pp: 1.7\n      k20_c0: 0.60\n      k20_tie_rate: 0.13\n      wall_time_hours: 4.5\n    result_rationale: \"C0 increases monotonically with K (more samples → more chance of diverse answers), but SICA-SC gap does not scale linearly. At K=6, too few traces means ties are rare (15%) and SC often has a clear majority — SICA has few opportunities to intervene. K=8 and K=12 hit the sweet spot: even K values create more exact ties, giving SICA maximum leverage. At K=16/20, C0 rises further but the majority winner becomes more stable (law of large numbers); ties actually decrease because the leading answer accumulates enough margin. The gap at K=20 shrinks because SC's majority is robust enough that tiebreaking matters less. This produces an inverted-U pattern peaking around K=8-12. Critically, odd K values (not tested here but inferrable) would show fewer ties, further supporting that SICA's primary mechanism is tiebreaking rather than general accuracy improvement.\"\n    tables:\n      - \"T3: K Sensitivity (K vs gap, C0, tie_rate — shows inverted-U peak at K=8-12)\"\n    figures_description:\n      - \"F4: K curve — x-axis K, dual y-axis showing SICA-SC gap (bars) and tie rate (line). Peak gap at K=8-12, declining at K=20.\"\n    analysis: \"The inverted-U supports the 'principled tiebreaker' narrative: SICA's value is mechanistically tied to tie frequency, not a general accuracy boost. Paper should emphasize K=8-12 as practical recommendation. The fact that gap doesn't grow unboundedly with K is actually a feature for the 'operating regime' story — it shows SICA's contribution is bounded and predictable.\"\n\n  - name: \"tiebreak_baseline_analysis\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-003\"\n    description: \"SICA vs SC-random vs SC-deterministic on all available data. CPU reanalysis.\"\n    expected_results:\n      n_tiebreak_cases_d012: 5\n      sica_tiebreak_accuracy: 0.80\n      random_tiebreak_accuracy_mean: 0.50\n      alphabetical_tiebreak_accuracy: 0.60\n      first_seen_tiebreak_accuracy: 0.40\n      n_tiebreak_cases_expanded: 68\n      sica_tiebreak_accuracy_expanded: 0.647\n      random_tiebreak_accuracy_expanded_mean: 0.485\n      sica_vs_random_p_expanded: 0.035\n      overall_sica_accuracy_with_random_baseline: 0.710\n      sica_minus_random_baseline_gap_pp: 0.8\n    result_rationale: \"On the original D012 5 ties, SICA's 80% (4/5) is consistent with pilot data. On expanded 68 ties, SICA's advantage regresses toward a more realistic 64.7% — still meaningfully above random's ~48.5% (random doesn't hit exactly 50% due to answer distribution asymmetry in the dataset). The critical comparison is: if SC uses random tiebreaking instead of alphabetical, it already captures some of the 'gap' attributed to SICA. The overall SICA-vs-SC-random gap shrinks to ~0.8pp (from 1.5pp vs deterministic), because random tiebreak is correct ~50% of the time on ties vs SICA's ~65%. This means roughly half of SICA's overall accuracy advantage comes from better tiebreaking specifically, and the other half from ties where SICA's constraint-weighted answer happens to differ from SC's majority (non-tie cases where MaxSAT exclusion changes the winner). The p=0.035 on tie-only comparison is the paper's cleanest statistical result.\"\n    tables:\n      - \"T2 extended: Tiebreak comparison table (SICA 64.7%, Random 48.5%, Alphabetical 52.9%, First-seen 45.6%)\"\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Bar chart comparing tiebreak strategies on 68 tie cases with 95% CI error bars\"\n    analysis: \"This is both the strongest and most modest result: SICA is a statistically significant better tiebreaker (p=0.035 on ties), but overall accuracy gain over a fair random baseline is small (~0.8pp). Paper must be honest about this decomposition. Reframe: 'SICA provides principled, interpretable tiebreaking with significant per-tie advantage, contributing a modest but consistent overall accuracy improvement.' The interpretability angle (you can inspect WHY SICA chose an answer via constraints) becomes the differentiator over random.\"\n\n  - name: \"sica_7b_scaling\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-004\"\n    description: \"Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct on D012 60 questions, K=12, T=0.7\"\n    expected_results:\n      sica_accuracy: 0.650\n      sc_accuracy: 0.617\n      sica_sc_gap_pp: 3.3\n      c0_multi_candidate_rate: 0.55\n      bootstrap_p: 0.18\n      folio_sica: 0.700\n      folio_sc: 0.633\n      folio_gap_pp: 6.7\n      proofwriter_sica: 0.619\n      proofwriter_sc: 0.607\n      proofwriter_gap_pp: 1.2\n      tiebreak_cases: 11\n      tiebreak_win_rate: 0.636\n      constraint_extraction_rate: 0.92\n      wall_time_hours: 1.8\n    result_rationale: \"7B is weaker than 14B (SC baseline drops from 70% to ~62%), producing more diverse outputs (C0 rises from 0.53 to 0.55). The C0 increase is real but modest — the 7B model is weaker but not dramatically so on these tasks; it still often converges on common answers. The SICA gap of 3.3pp matches the median expectation — higher C0 creates more intervention opportunities, but 7B's constraint extraction quality is lower (92% vs 100% for 14B), partially offsetting the diversity advantage. FOLIO shows the clearest gain (+6.7pp) because open-world NLI with a 3-class answer space maximizes tie probability for a confused model. However, bootstrap p=0.18 on 60 questions means none of this is individually significant — only the cross-model pattern matters. The key data point is: C0(7B)=0.55 > C0(14B)=0.53, confirming the directional scaling trend even if the magnitude is modest.\"\n    tables:\n      - \"T1: 7B row (FOLIO 70.0 vs 63.3, PW 61.9 vs 60.7, Combined 65.0 vs 61.7)\"\n    figures_description:\n      - \"F2 data point: (C0=0.55, gap=+3.3pp) — above and right of 14B point, confirms positive correlation\"\n    analysis: \"7B confirms 'weaker model → higher C0 → SICA helps more' directionally. But the C0 jump (0.53→0.55) is smaller than hoped — median tier says the scaling trend is noisy. The 7B point helps the F2 scatter plot but doesn't create a clean linear relationship. For the paper: include in T1 and F2, note that within-family scaling shows the expected direction but the gradient is shallow for adjacent sizes.\"\n\n  - name: \"qwq_32b_operating_regime\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-005\"\n    description: \"Format existing QwQ-32B data for Operating Regime Analysis section\"\n    expected_results:\n      c0_combined: 0.036\n      c0_aime: 0.0\n      c0_proofwriter: 0.1875\n      c0_folio: 0.0\n      sica_sc_gap_pp: 0.0\n      n_problems_total: 48\n      n_aime: 22\n      n_proofwriter: 16\n      n_folio: 10\n      reasoning_model_conclusion: \"C0<5% → SICA≡SC structurally\"\n    result_rationale: \"QwQ-32B's reasoning chains are so self-consistent that sampling temperature has negligible effect on final answers. The model's extended chain-of-thought process (often 2000+ tokens) acts as an internal consistency mechanism — by the time it reaches an answer, it has self-corrected any stochastic variation. C0=3.6% means only ~2 out of 48 problems produced any answer diversity at all, and even those had overwhelming majorities. SICA is structurally identical to SC because there are no ties to break and no minority answers for MaxSAT to potentially elevate. This is the 'degenerate regime' — the method's mechanism (resolving disagreement) has no disagreement to resolve. The key insight is that this is model-class-dependent (reasoning vs instruction), not task-dependent (same FOLIO/PW tasks show high C0 on 14B instruction model).\"\n    tables:\n      - \"T1: QwQ-32B row (all gaps = 0.0pp, C0 = 3.6%)\"\n    figures_description:\n      - \"F2 data point: (C0=0.036, gap=0.0pp) — bottom-left corner, clearly in 'degenerate regime'\"\n      - \"F3 heatmap: QwQ-32B occupies the C0<5% zone across all tested domains\"\n    analysis: \"This is Claim 2's strongest evidence in the paper. The contrast between 14B-Instruct (C0=53%, gap=+3.3pp) and QwQ-32B (C0=3.6%, gap=0pp) on the SAME tasks demonstrates that C0 is the governing variable, not task difficulty. Present as: 'reasoning models self-consistent → SICA redundant; instruction models diverse → SICA helps.' This frames the negative result as a positive insight about operating regimes.\"\n\n  - name: \"c0_diagnostic_validation\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-006\"\n    description: \"C0 vs SICA-SC gap scatter plot across all models and K values. CPU analysis.\"\n    expected_results:\n      n_data_points: 14\n      pearson_r: 0.58\n      spearman_rho: 0.64\n      c0_threshold_20_precision: 0.75\n      c0_threshold_20_recall: 1.0\n      c0_below_5_all_zero_gap: true\n      c0_above_20_positive_gap_rate: 0.75\n      binary_classification_accuracy: 0.857\n      auc_roc: 0.82\n    result_rationale: \"With ~14 data points (7B, 14B, 72B on 60q; 14B on 804q; QwQ on 48q; Mistral on 60q; LLaMA on 60q; DeepSeek-R1 on 60q; plus K-ablation variants), the correlation is positive but noisy (r=0.58). The noise comes from: (1) Mistral and LLaMA having high C0 but inconsistent gaps (one shows gain, one flat — median tier assumption), (2) K-ablation points clustering in a narrow C0 range, (3) PW subset having moderate C0 but near-zero gap (task formality confound). However, the BINARY diagnostic works well: C0<5% always means gap≈0 (reasoning models), and C0>20% means gap>0 in 75% of cases. The 25% failure rate (C0>20% but gap≈0) corresponds to cases where high diversity exists but constraint extraction quality is poor or the task doesn't benefit from symbolic reasoning (e.g., one cross-family model). Spearman (rank correlation) beats Pearson because the relationship is monotone but non-linear — there's a threshold effect rather than a smooth gradient.\"\n    tables:\n      - \"Appendix table: All (model, dataset, K, C0, gap) data points\"\n    figures_description:\n      - \"F2 (hero figure): Scatter plot C0 (x) vs gap (y) with color=model family, shape=reasoning/instruction. Two regime zones shaded: green (C0>20%, 'SICA regime') and red (C0<5%, 'degenerate regime'). Gray zone (5-20%) labeled 'uncertain.'\"\n    analysis: \"The binary diagnostic (C0>20% → try SICA, C0<5% → skip) is the paper's Claim 3 contribution. In median tier, it works as a diagnostic but NOT as a continuous predictor — the scatter has too much variance for a regression line to be useful. Paper should present the binary threshold clearly and acknowledge that within the 'SICA regime' (C0>20%), the magnitude of gain is task-dependent and not precisely predicted by C0 alone. This downgrades Claim 3 from 'continuous predictor' to 'binary diagnostic' as specified in the tier definition.\"\n\n  - name: \"sica_72b_awq\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-007\"\n    description: \"Qwen2.5-72B-Instruct AWQ 4-bit on D012 60 questions, K=12, T=0.7\"\n    expected_results:\n      sica_accuracy: 0.783\n      sc_accuracy: 0.767\n      sica_sc_gap_pp: 1.6\n      c0_multi_candidate_rate: 0.37\n      bootstrap_p: 0.35\n      folio_sica: 0.867\n      folio_sc: 0.833\n      folio_gap_pp: 3.4\n      proofwriter_sica: 0.738\n      proofwriter_sc: 0.738\n      proofwriter_gap_pp: 0.0\n      tiebreak_cases: 4\n      tiebreak_win_rate: 0.75\n      constraint_extraction_rate: 1.0\n      wall_time_hours: 3.8\n    result_rationale: \"72B is stronger than 14B (SC baseline 76.7% vs 70%), so it converges more often — but NOT dramatically more. C0=0.37 is below 14B's 0.53 but still well above the 20% threshold. In median tier, '72B not clearly below 14B' means the C0 drop is real but modest (0.53→0.37, not 0.53→0.15). This happens because 72B is still an instruction model — it doesn't have the reasoning model's self-consistency mechanism, just better initial accuracy. It still produces diverse outputs on genuinely ambiguous items. The SICA gap is small (1.6pp) because: (1) fewer ties with 72B's stronger majorities, (2) on PW, 72B is so accurate that there's no room for improvement. FOLIO still shows a gap (+3.4pp) because even 72B finds open-world NLI challenging enough to produce diverse traces. The scaling story becomes: 7B (C0=0.55) > 14B (C0=0.53) > 72B (C0=0.37) — directionally correct but the 7B-14B gap is tiny while the 14B-72B gap is larger. This is 'noisy' because you'd expect a more uniform gradient.\"\n    tables:\n      - \"T1: 72B row (FOLIO 86.7 vs 83.3, PW 73.8 vs 73.8, Combined 78.3 vs 76.7)\"\n    figures_description:\n      - \"F2 data point: (C0=0.37, gap=+1.6pp) — between 14B and QwQ, confirms within-family scaling\"\n    analysis: \"72B data creates the within-Qwen scaling dimension for the paper: 7B→14B→72B shows C0 decreasing and gap decreasing. But the pattern is noisy (7B and 14B are close, then 72B drops more). This is enough for a 'directional trend' claim but not a clean power law. Present as evidence that stronger instruction models approach but don't reach the reasoning model's degenerate regime. The 72B-AWQ quantization is a potential confound to acknowledge in limitations (4-bit may slightly increase randomness vs full precision).\"\n\n  - name: \"constraint_quality_case_studies\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-008\"\n    description: \"Qualitative analysis of constraints on 5-10 representative questions from exp-001\"\n    expected_results:\n      n_cases_analyzed: 8\n      semantically_meaningful_rate: 0.75\n      excluded_constraints_error_rate: 0.83\n      constraint_types: [\"entailment_chain\", \"negation\", \"universal_quantifier\", \"conditional\", \"disjunction\"]\n      avg_constraints_per_trace: 4.2\n      avg_unique_constraints_per_problem: 14.8\n      failure_modes: [\"over-literal parsing\", \"scope ambiguity in quantifiers\", \"implicit premises missed\"]\n    result_rationale: \"Constraint extraction from 14B traces is generally meaningful because FOLIO premises are already semi-formal (natural language with clear logical structure). ~75% of extracted constraints correctly capture the intended logical relationship. The 25% failures come from: (1) overly literal parsing of hedging language ('might', 'usually'), (2) quantifier scope ambiguity in nested sentences, (3) implicit common-sense premises that the model encodes but the extractor can't formalize. Excluded constraints (those MaxSAT deems unsatisfiable with the rest) correspond to reasoning errors ~83% of the time — this validates the MaxSAT mechanism as genuinely detecting flawed reasoning rather than randomly dropping constraints. The remaining 17% of exclusions are valid constraints that happen to conflict with the majority due to alternative valid interpretations.\"\n    tables: []\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Figure (appendix): 3 annotated examples showing constraint extraction → MaxSAT → answer selection pipeline on specific FOLIO questions\"\n    analysis: \"Case studies serve the interpretability narrative. Even if accuracy gains are modest, showing that MaxSAT exclusion corresponds to actual reasoning errors (83% of the time) validates the mechanism as principled rather than lucky. This supports reframing from 'accuracy tool' to 'interpretable reasoning aggregation.' Include 2-3 cherry-picked examples in main paper §4, full set in appendix.\"\n\n  - name: \"overhead_analysis\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-009\"\n    description: \"Wall-time breakdown per question from exp-001 logs\"\n    expected_results:\n      avg_total_time_per_question_s: 59.1\n      avg_generation_time_s: 48.3\n      avg_extraction_time_s: 8.4\n      avg_maxsat_time_s: 2.4\n      generation_fraction: 0.817\n      extraction_fraction: 0.142\n      maxsat_fraction: 0.041\n      overhead_vs_sc_percent: 22.4\n      sc_only_time_per_question_s: 48.3\n      batch_throughput_questions_per_hour: 61\n    result_rationale: \"Generation dominates (82%) because K=12 traces each require a full forward pass through the 14B model. Constraint extraction adds ~8.4s per question (LLM-based extraction of FOL from 12 traces, batched). MaxSAT solving is negligible (~2.4s, Z3 on small instances). Total overhead vs pure SC is ~22% — SC would take 48.3s (just generation), SICA adds 10.8s for extraction+solving. This is modest for a research prototype but worth noting. The extraction step could be parallelized (currently sequential over traces) or replaced with a smaller model, suggesting practical deployment could reduce overhead to <10%. Generation time dominates because vLLM batches efficiently but 12 traces × 14B parameters × ~500 output tokens each is substantial.\"\n    tables:\n      - \"T4: Overhead breakdown (Generation 48.3s/81.7%, Extraction 8.4s/14.2%, MaxSAT 2.4s/4.1%, Total overhead +22.4%)\"\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Stacked bar chart: time breakdown per stage, with annotation showing SC-only baseline\"\n    analysis: \"22% overhead is defensible for EMNLP — it's not 10x. Frame as: 'SICA adds modest overhead concentrated in constraint extraction (parallelizable), while MaxSAT solving is negligible. The primary cost is generation (shared with SC). Practical deployment with a dedicated extraction model could reduce overhead to <10%.' Reviewer concern addressed: the overhead IS worth it if you're in the C0>20% regime and want interpretable tiebreaking.\"\n\n  - name: \"sica_mistral_7b\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-010\"\n    description: \"Mistral-7B-Instruct-v0.3 on D012 60 questions, K=12, T=0.7\"\n    expected_results:\n      sica_accuracy: 0.583\n      sc_accuracy: 0.567\n      sica_sc_gap_pp: 1.6\n      c0_multi_candidate_rate: 0.35\n      bootstrap_p: 0.38\n      folio_sica: 0.600\n      folio_sc: 0.567\n      folio_gap_pp: 3.3\n      proofwriter_sica: 0.571\n      proofwriter_sc: 0.571\n      proofwriter_gap_pp: 0.0\n      tiebreak_cases: 6\n      tiebreak_win_rate: 0.667\n      constraint_extraction_rate: 0.85\n      wall_time_hours: 1.3\n    result_rationale: \"Mistral-7B is the 'one flat' family in the median tier's 'one shows gain, one flat' cross-family pattern. C0=0.35 confirms C0>20% holds outside Qwen (critical for Claim 2), but the SICA-SC gap is small and noisy (+1.6pp, p=0.38). This happens because Mistral's constraint extraction quality is lower (85% vs Qwen's 100%) — the model produces less structured reasoning traces, leading to more extraction failures and lower-quality constraints. When MaxSAT operates on noisy constraints, its tiebreaking advantage is partially offset by occasional wrong exclusions. FOLIO still shows a positive direction (+3.3pp) but PW is flat. The key takeaway is: C0 pattern generalizes (Mistral instruction model produces diverse outputs), but SICA's benefit depends on BOTH diversity AND extraction quality. Mistral's shorter, less structured traces hurt extraction.\"\n    tables:\n      - \"T1: Mistral-7B row (FOLIO 60.0 vs 56.7, PW 57.1 vs 57.1, Combined 58.3 vs 56.7)\"\n    figures_description:\n      - \"F2 data point: (C0=0.35, gap=+1.6pp) — in the 'SICA regime' by C0 but low gap, contributing to scatter\"\n    analysis: \"Mistral confirms C0>20% across families (critical) but shows that C0 is necessary-not-sufficient for SICA gains. The paper should acknowledge: 'Cross-family C0 pattern holds, but per-family SICA-SC delta varies with constraint extraction quality.' This is honest and actually strengthens the analysis paper framing — it identifies a second prerequisite beyond C0 (extraction quality). For the escalation decision: gap>0 but p>0.2 on 60 questions — per the plan note, could expand to 200+ questions, but median tier assumes we don't (GPU budget constraint or decision that the 60-question C0 confirmation is sufficient).\"\n\n  - name: \"sica_llama_8b\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-011\"\n    description: \"LLaMA-3.1-8B-Instruct on D012 60 questions, K=12, T=0.7\"\n    expected_results:\n      sica_accuracy: 0.617\n      sc_accuracy: 0.583\n      sica_sc_gap_pp: 3.4\n      c0_multi_candidate_rate: 0.40\n      bootstrap_p: 0.14\n      folio_sica: 0.667\n      folio_sc: 0.600\n      folio_gap_pp: 6.7\n      proofwriter_sica: 0.583\n      proofwriter_sc: 0.571\n      proofwriter_gap_pp: 1.2\n      tiebreak_cases: 9\n      tiebreak_win_rate: 0.667\n      constraint_extraction_rate: 0.93\n      wall_time_hours: 1.4\n    result_rationale: \"LLaMA-8B is the 'one shows gain' family in the median tier's cross-family pattern. C0=0.40 confirms diversity, and the gap (+3.4pp) is comparable to Qwen-14B's pilot result. LLaMA's instruction tuning produces reasonably structured outputs (extraction rate 93%), and at 8B parameters it's weak enough on FOLIO to produce genuine disagreement. The FOLIO gap of +6.7pp looks impressive but with only 30 FOLIO questions it's not significant (p=0.14 for combined). The key difference vs Mistral: LLaMA's traces are more structured (Meta's instruction tuning format includes clearer step markers), enabling better constraint extraction. This makes LLaMA the second 'positive signal' family alongside Qwen, while Mistral is the 'C0 holds but gains are flat' family. Together they support: 'C0 pattern generalizes; SICA benefit depends on both C0 and trace quality.'\"\n    tables:\n      - \"T1: LLaMA-8B row (FOLIO 66.7 vs 60.0, PW 58.3 vs 57.1, Combined 61.7 vs 58.3)\"\n    figures_description:\n      - \"F2 data point: (C0=0.40, gap=+3.4pp) — stronger signal than Mistral, closer to Qwen-14B's position\"\n    analysis: \"LLaMA provides the second positive cross-family confirmation. The 3-family narrative becomes: 'All three instruction-model families show C0>20% (operating regime confirmed), with two of three showing positive SICA-SC gaps and the third showing directional but flat improvement.' This is sufficient for EMNLP Findings — not every family needs significant gains if the C0 pattern is the primary claim. Paper frames Mistral's flat result as 'extraction quality moderates effectiveness' rather than as a failure.\"\n\n  - name: \"gsm8k_pilot_14b\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-012\"\n    description: \"Qwen2.5-14B on GSM8K 30 questions (K=12, T=0.7). Cross-domain C0 test.\"\n    expected_results:\n      sica_accuracy: 0.867\n      sc_accuracy: 0.867\n      sica_sc_gap_pp: 0.0\n      c0_multi_candidate_rate: 0.10\n      n_problems: 30\n      tiebreak_cases: 0\n      constraint_extraction_rate: 0.97\n      sc_baseline_accuracy: 0.867\n      wall_time_hours: 0.9\n    result_rationale: \"Qwen2.5-14B is strong on GSM8K (baseline ~87% with SC@12). The open-answer-space (numeric) combined with high model competence means the model almost always converges on the correct numerical answer. C0=10% — only 3 out of 30 questions produce any answer diversity, and those are the hardest items where the model sometimes makes arithmetic errors. With C0=10% (below 20% threshold but above 5%), this lands in the 'uncertain zone' — not quite degenerate but not clearly in SICA's operating regime. Zero ties means SICA has no mechanism to help. Gap is 0pp. This validates C0 as a cross-domain diagnostic: on tasks where the model is confident (high accuracy), C0 is low and SICA is unnecessary. The result also shows C0 reflects task-relative confidence, not answer-space properties (math has infinite answer space but low C0 when the model is competent).\"\n    tables:\n      - \"Appendix table: GSM8K pilot (C0=10%, gap=0pp, SC accuracy=86.7%)\"\n    figures_description:\n      - \"F2 data point: (C0=0.10, gap=0.0pp) — in the 'uncertain/low' zone, supporting C0 threshold narrative\"\n      - \"F3 heatmap: GSM8K cell in the 'C0<20%' region for 14B\"\n    analysis: \"GSM8K confirms that C0 is low when model competence is high, regardless of answer-space type. This is important for the paper's claim that C0 reflects 'task-relative model confidence.' Include in appendix as cross-domain validation. The result also pre-empts reviewer concern 'only works on restricted answer spaces' — we tested open-answer math and found C0 naturally low (model doesn't need help), which is exactly the predicted behavior.\"\n\n  - name: \"constraint_ablation\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-013\"\n    description: \"CPU ablation: SICA-MaxSAT vs SICA-count-only vs SICA-random-weight on exp-001 data\"\n    expected_results:\n      maxsat_accuracy_combined: 0.718\n      count_only_accuracy_combined: 0.711\n      random_weight_accuracy_combined: 0.706\n      maxsat_vs_countonly_gap_pp: 0.7\n      maxsat_vs_random_gap_pp: 1.2\n      countonly_vs_sc_gap_pp: 0.8\n      maxsat_folio_accuracy: 0.784\n      count_only_folio_accuracy: 0.770\n      maxsat_vs_countonly_folio_pp: 1.4\n      tiebreak_agreement_maxsat_countonly: 0.78\n      n_problems: 804\n    result_rationale: \"In the median tier, MaxSAT is marginally better than count-only but the delta is <2pp. This happens because on most tie cases, the constraint count already correlates with the correct answer (more constraints → more reasoning traces support it → more likely correct). MaxSAT adds value only when constraint QUALITY matters — i.e., when a few high-quality constraints for the correct answer outweigh many low-quality constraints for the wrong answer. On 804 questions with 68 ties, only ~15 cases show this pattern, yielding the marginal 0.7pp overall difference. Count-only still beats SC (by 0.8pp) because even raw constraint counting provides better tiebreaking than arbitrary rules. Random-weight performs worst because it destroys the informative signal entirely. The FOLIO subset shows the clearest MaxSAT advantage (+1.4pp over count-only) because formal-logic constraints have clearer satisfiability structure.\"\n    tables:\n      - \"Appendix table: Constraint Ablation (MaxSAT 71.8%, Count-only 71.1%, Random-weight 70.6%, SC 70.3%)\"\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Bar chart: accuracy by aggregation method with error bars, showing MaxSAT ≥ Count-only ≥ Random ≥ SC\"\n    analysis: \"MaxSAT is NOT essential for the core accuracy claim — count-only gets you 70% of the way there. But MaxSAT provides: (1) marginal additional accuracy, (2) interpretability (you can inspect which constraints were satisfied/excluded), (3) formal grounding (satisfiability checking is principled). Paper frames this as: 'MaxSAT provides interpretability and marginal accuracy gains over simpler alternatives. The primary contribution is the constraint-based framework and C0 diagnostic, not the specific solver.' This defuses the 'over-engineering' attack while preserving the intellectual contribution.\"\n\n  - name: \"sica_deepseek_r1_llama_8b\"\n    plan_id: \"exp-014\"\n    description: \"DeepSeek-R1-Distill-LLaMA-8B on D012 60 questions, K=12, T=0.7\"\n    expected_results:\n      sica_accuracy: 0.683\n      sc_accuracy: 0.683\n      sica_sc_gap_pp: 0.0\n      c0_multi_candidate_rate: 0.05\n      bootstrap_p: null\n      folio_sica: 0.700\n      folio_sc: 0.700\n      folio_gap_pp: 0.0\n      proofwriter_sica: 0.667\n      proofwriter_sc: 0.667\n      proofwriter_gap_pp: 0.0\n      tiebreak_cases: 0\n      tiebreak_win_rate: null\n      constraint_extraction_rate: 0.95\n      wall_time_hours: 1.8\n    result_rationale: \"DeepSeek-R1-Distill-LLaMA-8B is a reasoning model (distilled from R1) despite its smaller size. Like QwQ-32B, it has internalized extended chain-of-thought that produces self-consistent outputs. C0=5% (borderline, at the threshold) — only 3 out of 60 questions show any diversity, and those have overwhelming majorities. The key difference from QwQ-32B: DeepSeek-R1 has a LLaMA base architecture, eliminating the 'still Qwen family' attack on the QwQ data. Combined with QwQ-32B (C0=3.6%), this establishes: reasoning models as a CLASS show C0<5%, regardless of base architecture (Qwen or LLaMA) or scale (8B or 32B). SICA≡SC structurally. SC accuracy of 68.3% is higher than LLaMA-8B-Instruct's 58.3% despite same parameter count — reasoning distillation significantly improves accuracy while eliminating diversity. This is the clean 'reasoning vs instruction' contrast within the same architecture family.\"\n    tables:\n      - \"T1: DeepSeek-R1-LLaMA-8B row (all gaps = 0.0pp, C0 = 5%)\"\n    figures_description:\n      - \"F2 data point: (C0=0.05, gap=0.0pp) — at the boundary of 'degenerate regime', confirming reasoning model pattern\"\n      - \"F3 heatmap: DeepSeek-R1 occupies same low-C0 zone as QwQ-32B\"\n    analysis: \"DeepSeek-R1 is the second reasoning model confirmation. Together with QwQ-32B, establishes: reasoning models (any architecture, any scale) → C0<5% → SICA redundant. The contrast with LLaMA-8B-Instruct (same base, C0=40%, gap=+3.4pp) is particularly powerful: same architecture, instruction tuning vs reasoning distillation, completely different operating regime. This becomes a key comparison pair in the paper: 'LLaMA-8B-Instruct (C0=40%, +3.4pp) vs DeepSeek-R1-LLaMA-8B (C0=5%, 0pp) — same architecture, different training paradigm, opposite SICA behavior.'\"\n```","stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"825d69a6-8dd2-4a2c-bb74-4b8dca8f1b0e","total_cost_usd":0.38364375,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":13041,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"output_tokens":11525,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":13041,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":11525,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"cache_creation_input_tokens":13041,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":13041},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":8002,"outputTokens":16,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":0,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.008082,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":32000},"claude-opus-4-6":{"inputTokens":2,"outputTokens":11525,"cacheReadInputTokens":11841,"cacheCreationInputTokens"